date: 2026-09-10
subject: `jxl_simd::linear_rgb_to_xyb_batch` (the dispatched forward XYB SIMD
  kernel) vs `jxl_simd::forward_xyb_scalar` (the crate's own scalar fallback).
harness: a standalone crate path-depending ONLY on `jxl-encoder-simd`, so the
  measured code is the kernel and not a whole encode. 1 Mpx, min of 25, arms
  alternated inside each repeat with the leading arm rotated, threads=1.
  `jxl-encoder-simd/src/xyb.rs` verified sha256-identical on all four hosts
  before running (the i265 checkout reports crate version 0.3.2 but the kernel
  source matches, which is what is being measured).

  host     arch      cpu                          SIMD ms  scalar ms  SIMD/scalar
  mac      aarch64   Apple Silicon                  3.61      2.63       **1.37**
  dev      x86_64    Ryzen 9 9950X3D (Zen 5)        2.81     14.36        0.20
  r7900x   x86_64    Ryzen 9 7900X (Zen 4)          3.34     14.37        0.23
  i265     x86_64    Core Ultra 7 265K              2.60     13.99        0.19

  Bitwise differing pixels between the two arms: **0 of 1,048,576 on every
  host**, all three planes. The dispatch-parity claim holds, and it holds across
  architectures.

**THE FINDING DOES NOT GENERALISE, AND THAT IS THE POINT.** On x86 the SIMD
kernel is 4.3-5.3x FASTER than scalar on three different microarchitectures. On
aarch64 it is **1.37x SLOWER** than the crate's own scalar fallback, bit for bit.

So the aarch64-only opportunity is real — routing forward XYB to the scalar path
there would be a byte-identical ~27 % cut on a kernel that is 15.3 % of lossy e3
CPU (`e3_profile_2026-09-10.md`) — but the same change on x86 would be a **5x
regression on the primary platform**. This is precisely the case CLAUDE.md's
"a 512-bit kernel measured only on Zen 4 does not generalise" rule exists for; an
aarch64-only measurement here would have produced a confident, catastrophic
recommendation.

MECHANISM (consistent with the numbers, not separately proven): the kernel's
cube root runs two Newton iterations in **f64**, so it needs `f64x4`. On x86 that
is native. On aarch64 `f64x4` is a 2x`f64x2` polyfill and `f32x8` is a
2x`f32x4` polyfill, and the body additionally leaves the vector domain — it calls
`to_array()` on all three mixed vectors, runs the bit-hack initial guess in a
SCALAR loop over 24 lanes, rebuilds six `f64x4`s with `from_array`, and
reassembles through three `[f32; 8]` staging arrays. Doubly-polyfilled f64
division plus that round-trip is evidently worse than just writing the scalar
loop.

WHAT IS **NOT** BEING PROPOSED: a platform-specific dispatch change. One aarch64
machine (Apple Silicon) is not enough to change a tier list that also serves the
Windows ARM64 CI target and armv7, and the better fix probably subsumes it —
libjxl's division-free f32 cube root (`base/fast_math-inl.h::CubeRootAndAdd`)
would remove the f64 requirement entirely, which is what forces both the polyfill
AND the exclusion of the `v4`/AVX-512 tier. That route MOVES BYTES (max relative
disagreement 3.0e-7) and is an owner decision; see e3_profile_2026-09-10.md.

Measured alongside, same harness, aarch64, monomorphised so each arm is its own
branch-free loop: libjxl's division-free f32 cbrt costs **67-70 %** of our f64
two-divide cbrt in an identical loop, and the cube root is 86-87 % of the whole
transform.

MEASUREMENT TRAPS HIT AND FIXED ON THE WAY HERE — all three produced a confident
WRONG answer first:
  1. An arm writing ONE output plane instead of three made the SIMD kernel look
     35 % slower than scalar on aarch64. Store traffic must match.
  2. A `mode: u8` matched per pixel left a branch in the inner loop, stopped LLVM
     specialising, and made every arm measure the same (4.47 ms) regardless of
     what it computed. Monomorphise per arm.
  3. An `if x == 0.0 { return 0.0 }` early return in the libjxl cbrt transcription
     blocked auto-vectorisation; the Highway original uses a SELECT. With the
     branch removed the same arm went 4.47 -> 2.01 ms.
